(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-disorders-in-immunity-quiz"]={"title":"Disorders in Immunity — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why must ABO antigens be matched in transfusion?","choices":["Transfused cells are permanent replacements","There are no MHC molecules on red blood cells","MHC matching is more important than ABO for red cells","Red cells carry the full set of HLA antigens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so ABO is the critical matching system. You carry antibodies in your serum against the antigens you lack, and mismatch can be fatal through complement activation and lysis. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which predisposition to autoimmune conditions is described?","choices":["Women are affected more than men","Men are affected more than women","They do not run in families","They affect only one organ each"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Women are more likely to have autoimmune conditions than men, and autoimmunities also run in families. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which viruses are linked to the onset of type 1 diabetes?","choices":["Mumps virus and the measles virus","Coxsackie virus, echovirus and rubella","Hepatitis B virus and papillomavirus","Human herpesvirus 8 and Epstein-Barr virus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coxsackie viruses A and B, echovirus and rubella are listed as triggers of type 1 diabetes through a normal immune response. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which are named as infectious allergy examples of type IV?","choices":["Tuberculosis, leprosy, syphilis and histoplasmosis","Hay fever and anaphylaxis","Transfusion reactions and hemolytic disease of the newborn","Serum sickness and the Arthus reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with toxoplasmosis and candidiasis completing the list — chronic infections where the T cell response itself damages tissue. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A young boy has recurrent bacterial infections and no detectable antibodies. Which defect is most likely?","choices":["A T cell defect","An acquired viral infection","A mast cell defect","A B cell defect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Agammaglobulinemia is a B cell defect; the patient lacks antibodies and suffers more bacterial infections. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which are the general targets of a type I reaction?","choices":["Bone marrow and lymph nodes","Liver and spleen","Joints and kidneys","Skin, airway, gut and conjunctiva"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, producing rashes, itching, redness, rhinitis, sneezing, diarrhea and tears respectively. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the Arthus reaction?","choices":["Localized dermal injury from inflamed blood vessels","A systemic reaction to foreign animal protein","A delayed T cell response to an injected antigen","An immediate wheal and flare"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a vasculitis, typically following a second injection of vaccine at a site, and normally self limiting. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Bacillus Calmette-Guérin is used to treat which cancer?","choices":["Kidney cancer","Bladder cancer","Prostate cancer","Cervical cancer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bacillus Calmette-Guérin, a weakened form of the tuberculosis organism, stimulates a general immune response and is used to treat bladder cancer. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A surgeon harvests a vein from a patient's own leg to bypass a blocked artery. Which graft type is this?","choices":["An allograft","A xenograft","An autograft","An isograft"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An autograft is a self graft; skin, gum and vein grafts moved within one patient are the examples given. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What are the consequences of a transfusion reaction?","choices":["Red cell destruction, glomerular blockage, fever and jaundice","Immune complex deposition in joints and skin","Delayed T cell infiltration of the transfused tissue","Bronchial smooth muscle contraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and even where complement activation is incomplete, antibody-coated cells may attract natural killer cells or macrophages. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which hypersensitivity types are B cell mediated?","choices":["Types I, II and III","Types I and II only","Type IV only","All four types"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and type IV is the exception — it is T cell mediated, which is why it is delayed rather than immediate. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How many IgE molecules can each mast cell bind?","choices":["Between 10,000 and 40,000","Between 10 and 40","Exactly one","Between 100 and 400"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which explains the scale of the response — a single cell carries an enormous number of trigger points for crosslinking. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Premature aging of the thymus is characteristic of which group?","choices":["Young people with autoimmune disorders","Adults who received bone marrow grafts","Children with helminth infections","Mothers sensitized to the Rh antigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Premature thymic aging is characteristic of young people with autoimmune disorders, and chronic inflammation may hasten it. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How do immune checkpoint inhibitors work?","choices":["They kill cancer cells directly","They return engineered T cells","They infect and lyse tumor cells","They block the T cell off signal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They stop checkpoint proteins binding their partners, preventing the off signal so T cells keep killing cancer cells. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What does the hygiene hypothesis propose?","choices":["Exposure to allergens in childhood causes lifelong allergy","Hygiene practices directly damage epithelial barriers","Vaccination produces IgE against vaccine components","Underused immune systems are less successfully regulated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Better hygiene, vaccination and antibiotics eradicated helminth and other parasitic infections in Western Europe and North America, leaving the system without its usual training. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are the systemic targets of a type I reaction?","choices":["Red blood cells and platelets","Basement membranes of epithelial tissue","Smooth muscle, mucous glands, nerve","Skin, conjunctiva and nasal mucosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the responses are vascular dilation and constriction, producing changes in blood pressure and respiration. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Graft versus host disease is which hypersensitivity type?","choices":["Type III","Type IV","Type I","Type II"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Graft versus host disease is type IV: donor T cells in the graft attack the recipient's tissues. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is immune surveillance?","choices":["Tissue typing before a transplant","Skin prick testing for allergies","Immunity keeping cancer in check","Screening blood donors for ABO type"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immune surveillance is the immune system keeping cancer in check, which is why its failure allows tumors to establish. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What does prevention of allergy involve?","choices":["Taking antihistamines daily regardless of exposure","Injecting small amounts of the allergen","Administering epinephrine prophylactically","Modifying environment and behavior"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is listed first because removing the trigger is the only approach that does not depend on blunting a normal mechanism. The aim is to avoid the allergen entirely. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Hepatitis B virus is associated with which cancer?","choices":["Kaposi's sarcoma","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Cervical carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hepatitis B virus is associated with liver cancer, hepatocellular carcinoma, which is why its vaccine is a prophylactic cancer vaccine. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the most common form of allergen specific immunotherapy?","choices":["Inhaled allergen challenge","Subcutaneous immunotherapy","Sublingual immunotherapy","Intravenous allergen infusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — small amounts of allergen introduced by injection, improving reactions in about 80 percent of those who complete the process. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"An infant with an absent thymus has recurrent fungal and viral infections. Which condition fits?","choices":["Graft versus host disease","Hashimoto's thyroiditis","DiGeorge syndrome","Agammaglobulinemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. DiGeorge syndrome is a T cell defect with a missing or abnormal thymus, and it brings fungal, protozoan, helminth and viral infections. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which is a possible cause of cancer?","choices":["Overactive thymic selection","Activation of oncogenes","Loss of immunoglobulin E","Excess regulatory T cell death"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Errors in mitosis, genetic damage, activation of oncogenes and retroviruses are the possible causes listed; all disrupt the normal cell division cycle. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which four categories of immunopathology are described?","choices":["Bacterial, viral, fungal and parasitic disease","Innate, adaptive, humoral and cellular","Allergy, autoimmunity, immunodeficiency and cancer","Infection, inflammation, necrosis and repair"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they sort neatly by direction: allergy and autoimmunity are overreactions, while immunodeficiency and cancer are underreactions. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Reactive arthritis follows infection with which organisms?","choices":["Coxsackie virus, echovirus and rubella","Borrelia burgdorferi and group A strep","Epstein-Barr virus and a retrovirus","Shigella, Salmonella and Campylobacter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reactive arthritis is an autoimmune result of a normal immune response to Shigella, Salmonella or Campylobacter. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How many people are heavily and persistently infected with helminths?","choices":["About ten thousand","Fewer than one hundred thousand","About one billion","About one million"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, in countries where helminth infection is endemic — and notably those populations do not show corresponding allergic disease. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A child develops arthritis and myocarditis after a streptococcal infection. What is the mechanism?","choices":["Streptococci directly invade heart muscle","Donor T cells attack the child's joints","A missing thymus leaves T cells unchecked","Strep antibodies cross-react with heart"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is rheumatic fever: the streptococcal cell wall stimulates antibodies, and some cross-react with heart tissue, a molecular mimicry reaction. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What does a high number of regulatory T cells in a tumor predict?","choices":["Imminent graft rejection","A worse prognosis","A better prognosis","No change in prognosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Regulatory T cells release transforming growth factor beta and interleukin 10, suppressing tumor-specific T cells; the more there are, the worse the prognosis. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary immunodeficiency from a secondary one?","choices":["It always follows a viral infection","It involves only the complement system","It is congenital, usually genetic","It is acquired after birth from a drug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary immunodeficiency is congenital and usually genetic; secondary immunodeficiency is acquired after birth from natural or artificial agents. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is a graft taken from a genetically identical twin called?","choices":["An isograft","An autograft","An allograft","A xenograft"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An isograft comes from a genetically identical twin, so donor and recipient share the same histocompatibility antigens. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 43"}]};
